<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang='en-US'><head><meta content="text/html; charset=UTF-8" http-equiv="Content-Type"/><script>window['ec:pageId']='17057840380036462135'</script><title>CFP</title><link href="/images/favicon.ico" rel="icon" type="image/jpeg"/><link href="/css/cool.css?version=541" rel="StyleSheet" type="text/css"/>
<link href="/css/cfp/classic.css?version=541" rel="StyleSheet" type="text/css"/>
<link href="/css/menu_cfp.css?version=541" rel="StyleSheet" type="text/css"/>
<script src="/js/easy.js?version=541"></script><script src="/js/menu.js?version=541"></script><script src="/js/form.js?version=541"></script><script src="/js/inplace.js?version=541"></script><script>EC.version='541'</script></head>
<body><table class="page" id="page"><tr style="height:1px"><td><table class="header"><tr><td class="logocell"><a href="/" title="EasyChair home page"><img class="leftlogo" src="/images/logoEC_short.png"/></a></td><td class="rolecell">EasyChair Smart CFP</td><td style="width: 99%"></td><td class="helplink"><a class="toplinks" href="/docs/home" title="User guide">User guide</a></td></tr></table></td></tr><tr style="height:99%"><td class="menu_and_content"><div class="menu" id="menu0"><table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" class="menu" style="position:static; visibility:visible"><tr><td class="top" id="menu1" onclick="Menu.followLink2('menu1','/cfp',{&quot;a&quot;:null})" onmouseout="Menu.hide('menu1')" onmouseover="Menu.show('menu1')" title="Main page">Home</td><td class="top" id="menu2" onclick="Menu.followLink2('menu2','/cfp/choose',{})" onmouseout="Menu.hide('menu2')" onmouseover="Menu.show('menu2')" title="Create a new CFP">New CFP</td><td class="top" id="menu3" onclick="Menu.followLink2('menu3','/cfp/all',{})" onmouseout="Menu.hide('menu3')" onmouseover="Menu.show('menu3')" title="Manage my CFPs">My CFPs</td><td class="top" id="menu4" onclick="Menu.followLink2('menu4','/cfp/watchlist',{})" onmouseout="Menu.hide('menu4')" onmouseover="Menu.show('menu4')" title="My CFP watchlist">Watchlist</td></tr></table></div><div class="search_row" id="cfp:search:row"><form action="/cfp/search" id="search-form"><input id="search-input" name="query" placeholder="Search" type="text"/></form></div><div class="summary"><div class="pagetitle">CFP</div></div><div class="content" id="content"><div class="subcontent"><div id="cfp" style="clear:right"><table class="title_table"><tr><td class="single"><div id="cfptitle">SovereignAI 2026: The First Workshop on Sovereign AI for Collaborative and Pluralistic AI Ecosystems</div><div id="cfpvenue">Virginia Tech Academic Building One, 3625 Potomac Avenue</div><div id="cfpdateplace">Alexandria, VA, United States, September 27, 2026</div></td></tr></table><table class="date_table"><tr><td>Conference web page</td><td><a href="https://sovereignai-workshop.github.io/" target="_blank">https://sovereignai-workshop.github.io/</a></td></tr><tr><td>Submission link</td><td><a href="https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=sovereignai2026" target="_blank">https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=sovereignai2026</a></td></tr><tr><td>Abstract registration deadline</td><td>August 24, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Submission deadline</td><td>August 31, 2026</td></tr></table><div class="topics">Topics: <a href="/cfp/topic?tid=17061" onclick="return EC.linkClick(event)"><span class="tag fg_violet bg_darkyellow">nlp</span></a> <a href="/cfp/topic?tid=17314" onclick="return EC.linkClick(event)"><span class="tag fg_blue bg_cobalt">hci</span></a> <a href="/cfp/topic?tid=18115" onclick="return EC.linkClick(event)"><span class="tag fg_red bg_yellow">ai</span></a> <a href="/cfp/topic?tid=4247" onclick="return EC.linkClick(event)"><span class="tag fg_navy bg_ros">policy</span></a></div><p><span style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif">We invite contributors across five broad themes, welcoming perspectives from AI, HCI, and public policy.</span></p><p><span style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif">Each theme is introduced by an illustrative question, but the questions and topics listed below are intended to serve as starting points rather than limits. We also encourage submissions that go beyond these themes by extending, reframing, or connecting them.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><strong style="font-weight:700"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">1. Safety and Alignment for Agentic &amp; Physical AI</span></span></strong></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">How do we ensure that increasingly autonomous and embodied AI systems remain safe, aligned, and under meaningful human control, especially when they are adapted or deployed outside the contexts in which they were built?</span></span></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><em style="font-style:italic"><span style="text-decoration:none">Possible topics: agentic and embodied AI, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), multimodal alignment, human-in-the-loop, safety evaluation, LLM vulnerabilities, scalable oversight, uncertainty estimation and calibration, vision-language-action (VLA) models</span></em></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><strong style="font-weight:700"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">2. Open Models, Data Sovereignty, and Responsible Deployment</span></span></strong></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38; margin-bottom:16px; margin-top:16px"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">How can open models and local data governance move beyond the defensive goal of "reducing dependence" to become positive conditions for cultural and narrative agency? How can communities build on global models while retaining control over how their data, corpora, and knowledge are collected, interpreted, and used?</span></span></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38; margin-bottom:16px; margin-top:16px"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><em style="font-style:italic"><span style="text-decoration:none">Possible topics: Indigenous and community data sovereignty, open-weight and foundation models, local adaptation, data provenance</span></em></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><strong style="font-weight:700"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">3. Human Agency &amp; Trustworthy Human-AI Interaction </span></span></strong></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">How can people effectively understand, oversee, and intervene in AI systems without losing their own agency? </span></span></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><em style="font-style:italic"><span style="text-decoration:none">Possible topics: human-AI and human-robot interaction, explainable AI(XAI), delegation, mixed-initiative interaction, appropriate reliance, usable transparency and privacy, user control, contestability, human intervention, and failure recovery</span></em></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><strong style="font-weight:700"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">4. Inclusive AI for Underserved Communities</span></span></strong></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">How can people from different technical, cultural, and professional backgrounds help build AI that better serves diverse languages, communities, and public needs?</span></span></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><em style="font-style:italic"><span style="text-decoration:none">Possible topics: multilingual and low-resource NLP, culturally grounded AI, community-driven data and models, accessibility, civic technologies, news and information integrity, media literacy, and resource-efficient deployment</span></em></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><strong style="font-weight:700"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">5. Democratic Resilience and Multi-Stakeholder AI Governance</span></span></strong></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><span style="font-style:normal"><span style="text-decoration:none">How can governments, researchers, industry, and the public meaningfully participate in governing AI and holding it accountable? And when "the local community" is itself plural — even internally contested — who has the legitimacy to speak on its behalf?</span></span></span></span></span></span></p><p style="line-height:1.38"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-variant:normal; white-space:pre-wrap"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><span style="color:#000000"><span style="font-weight:400"><em style="font-style:italic"><span style="text-decoration:none">Possible topics: agile regulatory design, AI ethics, AI law and privacy, public accountability, transparency and explainability, auditable algorithms, due process and contestability, institutional coordination, public-sector deployment, co-governance, and deliberative democracy</span></em></span></span></span></span></p></div></div></div></td></tr><tr><td class="footer">Copyright © 2002 – 2026 EasyChair</td></tr></table></body></html>
